1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:05,750
Linear regression is worth more points than any other topic on these practice

2
00:00:05,750 --> 00:00:07,355
midterms: 62 out of 300.

3
00:00:07,355 --> 00:00:12,572
The good news is that almost every question is a reading exercise on R output.

4
00:00:12,572 --> 00:00:16,585
Here's how to read it, and the moves the exam keeps testing.

5
00:00:18,200 --> 00:00:23,356
Here's the output from practice midterm two: a regression predicting a CPU's

6
00:00:23,356 --> 00:00:26,884
published performance, PRP, from six hardware specs.

7
00:00:26,884 --> 00:00:30,072
Three parts of it answer nearly every question.

8
00:00:30,853 --> 00:00:35,774
The Estimate column is the coefficient: how much the prediction changes when that

9
00:00:35,774 --> 00:00:38,933
feature rises by one unit, holding the others fixed.

10
00:00:38,933 --> 00:00:41,363
That's what you use to make predictions.

11
00:00:42,120 --> 00:00:48,396
The last column is the p-value for the null hypothesis that the coefficient is zero.

12
00:00:48,396 --> 00:00:50,563
Below 0.05 means significant.

13
00:00:50,563 --> 00:00:54,224
The stars are just a shortcut for the same thing.

14
00:00:54,987 --> 00:00:59,095
And R squared at the bottom is the fit on the training data only.

15
00:00:59,095 --> 00:01:02,762
Adjusted R squared adds a penalty for each extra variable,

16
00:01:02,762 --> 00:01:05,733
so use it to compare models of different sizes.

17
00:01:07,333 --> 00:01:09,895
Predictions are plug and chug.

18
00:01:09,895 --> 00:01:16,984
On practice midterm two, a new CPU has a cycle time of 75, minimum memory of 2,500,

19
00:01:16,984 --> 00:01:22,792
maximum memory of 10,000, cache of 30, and between 4 and 8 channels.

20
00:01:23,553 --> 00:01:28,021
Start with the intercept, then add each coefficient times its value.

21
00:01:28,021 --> 00:01:33,146
Write the whole expression before you compute: the rubric gives points for the

22
00:01:33,146 --> 00:01:37,746
right coefficients and the right values, even if the arithmetic slips.

23
00:01:37,746 --> 00:01:39,454
The answer is about 81.67.

24
00:01:40,220 --> 00:01:46,410
Same move on midterm three's golf model gives about 2.64 million dollars.

25
00:01:46,410 --> 00:01:50,056
One trap: driving accuracy is a percentage,

26
00:01:50,056 --> 00:01:56,160
and the data preview shows values like 57.7, so you plug in 60, not 0.6.

27
00:01:57,767 --> 00:02:01,670
Significance questions want a verdict plus evidence.

28
00:02:01,670 --> 00:02:03,021
Use this template.

29
00:02:03,021 --> 00:02:08,125
Yes or no: the p-value is this number, which is below or above 0.05,

30
00:02:08,125 --> 00:02:13,830
so we can or cannot reject the null hypothesis that the coefficient is zero.

31
00:02:14,587 --> 00:02:21,292
Average score on the golf model has a p-value of 2.46 times 10 to the minus 15:

32
00:02:21,292 --> 00:02:22,311
significant.

33
00:02:22,311 --> 00:02:27,573
Average putts has 0.617, and CHMAX on the CPU model has 0.179,

34
00:02:27,573 --> 00:02:29,780
so neither is significant.

35
00:02:29,780 --> 00:02:31,223
Quote the number.

36
00:02:31,223 --> 00:02:33,600
That's where the points are.

37
00:02:35,200 --> 00:02:37,620
Next, R squared versus OSR squared.

38
00:02:37,620 --> 00:02:40,662
R squared measures fit on the training data.

39
00:02:40,662 --> 00:02:43,428
OSR squared measures it on the test set,

40
00:02:43,428 --> 00:02:46,954
compared against just predicting the training mean.

41
00:02:47,720 --> 00:02:51,164
Adding a variable can never lower training R squared,

42
00:02:51,164 --> 00:02:53,504
and removing one can never raise it.

43
00:02:53,504 --> 00:02:56,234
But removal can leave R squared unchanged,

44
00:02:56,234 --> 00:03:01,628
so the claim on practice midterm three that removing a variable always decreases it

45
00:03:01,628 --> 00:03:02,212
is false.

46
00:03:02,987 --> 00:03:04,912
Out of sample, anything goes.

47
00:03:04,912 --> 00:03:09,426
Test OSR squared can rise or fall when you add or remove a variable,

48
00:03:09,426 --> 00:03:13,808
so a higher training R squared guarantees nothing on the test set.

49
00:03:13,808 --> 00:03:19,052
OSR squared can even be negative, which means the model predicts worse than the

50
00:03:19,052 --> 00:03:19,982
training mean.

51
00:03:20,753 --> 00:03:25,558
One more true or false: with two points at different x values and no noise,

52
00:03:25,558 --> 00:03:30,555
least squares fits them exactly, so the estimates equal the true coefficients.

53
00:03:30,555 --> 00:03:30,875
True.

54
00:03:32,467 --> 00:03:37,513
Multicollinearity means one feature is nearly a combination of the others.

55
00:03:37,513 --> 00:03:41,945
The VIF measures it: regress feature j on all the other features,

56
00:03:41,945 --> 00:03:45,696
take that R squared, and compute one over one minus it.

57
00:03:45,696 --> 00:03:46,787
Above 5 is high.

58
00:03:46,787 --> 00:03:51,219
It inflates standard errors and makes coefficient signs unstable.

59
00:03:51,987 --> 00:03:56,035
On practice midterm two, the largest VIF is 3.14,

60
00:03:56,035 --> 00:03:59,587
so the answer is no high multicollinearity,

61
00:03:59,587 --> 00:04:04,792
even though MMIN and MMAX have a pairwise correlation near 0.8.

62
00:04:04,792 --> 00:04:07,848
The VIF is the test the rubric wants.

63
00:04:08,620 --> 00:04:14,797
And the true or false from midterm one: can variables have large VIFs but only

64
00:04:14,797 --> 00:04:17,093
modest pairwise correlations?

65
00:04:17,093 --> 00:04:17,410
Yes.

66
00:04:17,410 --> 00:04:21,607
If X nine is roughly the sum of eight other features,

67
00:04:21,607 --> 00:04:27,704
each pairwise correlation is only about 0.35, but X nine's VIF is around 800.

68
00:04:29,300 --> 00:04:34,034
A favorite short answer: can adding this new variable improve the model?

69
00:04:34,034 --> 00:04:37,651
If it's a linear combination of existing variables, no.

70
00:04:37,651 --> 00:04:41,531
CHAVG, the average of CHMIN and CHMAX, and putts per round,

71
00:04:41,531 --> 00:04:46,725
which is 18 times putts per hole, add nothing the model couldn't already build.

72
00:04:46,725 --> 00:04:51,394
R would even report an NA coefficient, because of perfect collinearity.

73
00:04:52,153 --> 00:04:55,398
If it's a nonlinear transformation, yes, it can help.

74
00:04:55,398 --> 00:05:00,113
Cache squared, a log, or a product of two features gives the model a shape it

75
00:05:00,113 --> 00:05:01,399
couldn't make before.

76
00:05:03,000 --> 00:05:07,744
Midterm three also shows two scatter plots: winnings against average score,

77
00:05:07,744 --> 00:05:10,654
and the log of winnings against average score.

78
00:05:10,654 --> 00:05:13,943
Raw winnings curve upward and fan out at low scores.

79
00:05:13,943 --> 00:05:17,233
The log version is a straight band with even spread.

80
00:05:17,987 --> 00:05:22,017
So choose log of winnings: linear regression fits straight lines,

81
00:05:22,017 --> 00:05:24,621
and the log makes the relationship linear.

82
00:05:24,621 --> 00:05:29,644
Just remember to predict on the log scale, then exponentiate to get dollars back.

83
00:05:31,233 --> 00:05:32,699
Last, a reading trap.

84
00:05:32,699 --> 00:05:37,443
A coefficient compares players with every other variable held fixed,

85
00:05:37,443 --> 00:05:40,792
so its sign can differ from the raw correlation.

86
00:05:40,792 --> 00:05:46,374
In the golf data, average putts correlates negatively with winnings, minus 0.28,

87
00:05:46,374 --> 00:05:50,491
but its coefficient is positive, 2.19, and not significant.

88
00:05:50,491 --> 00:05:54,049
Holding average score fixed changes the comparison.

89
00:05:55,633 --> 00:05:56,259
To recap.

90
00:05:56,259 --> 00:06:01,678
A prediction is the intercept plus each coefficient times its value: write the

91
00:06:01,678 --> 00:06:04,040
expression first, and check units.

92
00:06:04,040 --> 00:06:08,209
A coefficient is significant when its p-value is below 0.05,

93
00:06:08,209 --> 00:06:10,571
and you should quote that p-value.

94
00:06:10,571 --> 00:06:14,323
Training R squared can't fall when you add a variable,

95
00:06:14,323 --> 00:06:16,894
but test OSR squared can do anything.

96
00:06:16,894 --> 00:06:21,062
Judge multicollinearity with the VIF, where above 5 is high.

97
00:06:21,062 --> 00:06:26,134
Linear combinations of features never help, but nonlinear transforms can.

98
00:06:26,134 --> 00:06:30,094
And if the relationship curves and fans out, try the log.

99
00:06:30,094 --> 00:06:32,595
Next: logistic regression and L D A.
